mean tokens per role identifier                   7.83   fighting the surface
mean tokens per opaque relation token              3.4   ok

VERDICT: usable but costly: role identifiers average 7.83 tokens, so correct emission is that many consecutive correct choices. A grammar mask is not an optimization here, it is the mechanism

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE PRETRAINING RUN (the part that cannot be redone):
  1. Run this against the CANDIDATE vocabulary before the run, not after.
     Nothing here is specific to one tokenizer; --tokenizer / --gguf swaps it.
  2. The program surface round-trips, so the vocabulary is not disqualified.
     Depth is not a context problem either: a 10-hop program is ~258 tokens
     against a 700 budget.
  3. The expensive thing is the ROLE IDENTIFIER, and it is expensive because
     the pre-tokenizer splits on `_` before BPE ever runs -- `H1_DATE_OF_BIRTH`
     is 10 tokens. More merges will not fix that; it is structural.
     Two independent ways out, and they are the same change:
       - WO-2.2's grammar makes the wrong continuation undecodable, so the
         fumble cannot happen however many steps it takes
       - WO-2.7's role-as-VECTOR convention (hash the role name into a
         hypervector instead of emitting an identifier) means those 10
         tokens are never emitted at all
     WO-0.3 wanted the second one for generalization -- the role vocabulary is
     95-98% per-relation, which caps the system at its training relations.
     This says it also buys ~8 decode steps per hop. Same change, two reasons.